welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is James Levinson the Charles W Goodyear  professor in global affairs and  professor of economics and management  and the founding director of the Jackson  Institute for global affairs at Yale  University as the Jackson Institute's  first director professor Levinson brings  a wealth of international experience to  that post his fields of interest include  international economics industrial  organization economic development and  applied econometrics recently he has  studied the impact of hiv/aids on  unemployment and school attendance in  South Africa  professor Levinson has lived and worked  in Senegal Botswana and South Africa one  of his projects now in its 11th year  trans government officials University  faculty and students and NGO staff from  over a dozen countries in South Africa  on how to use data to inform  policymaking today we'll talk with  Professor Levinson about the new Jackson  Institute for global affairs  welcome professor Levinson pleased to be  here the Jackson Institute just opened  its doors a month ago in September  congratulations right let's talk a  little bit about it and its mission so  we really have three missions they're  all interrelated  our first task if you will is that we  are the home of the undergraduate major  International Studies and the master's  degree in international relations those  two degree programs are the focus of our  teaching we also offer courses that are  open to all students in Yale College so  we have some service courses as well as  courses for our majors the second thing  that we've really focused on is bringing  practitioners there senior fellows at  the Jackson Institute these are very  distinguished  people who have had careers in global  affairs bringing them into the classroom  Yale is just chock-full of wonderful  speakers on any given afternoon and my  goal is to get people to come and to  engage not with an afternoon lecture but  teaching an entire semester or at least  several weeks and we have a number of  these Jackson fellows who are currently  on campus and teaching courses okay I'm  sorry no no the third thing that we  focused on I mentioned there were three  is offering career services to Yale  students but with a special focus on  students who are interested in what you  might think of as non-traditional or non  business oriented careers abroad careers  in diplomacy careers in the State  Department careers in government careers  with NGOs so we have that office up and  running also very good let's talk a  little bit about the first part of you  know the students and and the classwork  your training students basically to be  future leaders how are you going about  doing that we're doing that by trying to  give them the tools they need in a world  that we don't really know what it would  look like so we're trying to give them a  tool box trying to teach courses that  use economics that use political science  courses that draw on the experiences of  the Jackson fellows so that they will be  able to succeed in a pretty agile way  and respond to a marketplace that quite  honestly I have no idea what it will  look like five ten years from now the  senior fellows as you mentioned are  heavy hitters one of them I know is  General Stanley McChrystal tell us a  little bit more about the fellows and  specifically and how you chose them so  we have a wide range and we wanted to  get a group of senior fellows who were  quite obviously distinguished but bring  different things to the table so you  mentioned general McChrystal Stan  McChrystal is teaching seminar on  leadership something he's very very well  qualified to do I've sat in on the first  class or two it's fascinating  just fascinating about 20 students a mix  of mostly graduate students but we saved  about six or seven slots for  undergraduates so he speaks on issues of  leadership and he has a group of people  coming in talking about leadership what  what makes for an effective leader  leadership in an age of 24/7 media  coverage he has a fascinating syllabus  some of the other ones some of the  others so what we have Steve Roach Steve  is a former president or chairman of  Morgan Stanley Asia in the world of Wall  Street economists he's a rock star Steve  is teaching a course titled the next  China most of our courses we expected to  have maybe if we were lucky 50 60  students I think Steve has something  like 200 250 students and he's dry on  his experiences both as an economist and  as a business person who's been working  in Asia for the last many years also on  the economic side Rakesh Mohan is senior  fellow Rakesh was the deputy governor of  the Reserve Bank of India  sometimes when I'm describing this to  friends I just say well he's the Ben  Bernanke of India he is it's really fun  to have an economist teaching a course  not so much drawing on a textbook  although Rakesh as a distinguished PhD  economists but he's also the guy who  helps set interest rates for 1.2 billion  people thought about these things okay  so you have China covered India covered  what about the Middle East Middle East  is fascinating we're thrilled to have  Marwan MA sure as a senior fellow Marwan  is the former deputy president of Jordan  he was Jordans first ambassador to  Israel and former Jordanian ambassador  to the United States he's doing a course  on Middle East peace has a wonderful  simulation exercise it's a great class  your experience your extensive  experience out in the world I'm sure is  going to be influencing  what you do at the Jackson Institute how  how are you bringing your personal  experiences in terms of shaping how the  Jackson Institute grows so I'm actually  gonna try to not bring my personal  experiences at least too heavily to bear  I'm only one person and there are many  different and fascinating career paths  I've done a fair amount of work in  developing countries and that will be  one of the areas of focus for the  Jackson Institute I've been recently  working in the field of global health as  well as economics and we will be  offering a number of courses in in that  field I think there's a lot of interest  and a lot of expertise on this campus in  the field of global health we there's  wonder they're wonderful programs at the  graduate level but undergraduates  haven't traditionally had as much ask as  much access to those courses to that  field yet the interest is there so we'll  be adding courses in global health  courses in economic development courses  in development more broadly what are the  institutions required for countries to  thrive but for example I have virtually  no experience in the world of diplomacy  yet that's one of the areas of focus of  the Jackson Institute we have multiple  diplomats who are coming or are in  residence and I think that'll be fun too  wonderful and you are teaching a course  for undergraduates that I hear is very  popular gateway to global affairs tell  us a little bit about it I understand  there's four parts to it and I'm  wondering how how you chose the themes  for the class so this class is fun it's  we've chosen as you noted four topics  the first topic was u.s. immigration  policy what should immigration policy be  what are some of the forces that come to  play in thinking about the politics as  well as the economics of it and for that  module I think of these four topics as  comprising modules of the course for  that module Gordon Hanson an expert on  immigration policy from the University  of California San Diego came and joined  me  I taught the part on globalization he  taught the part on immigration was fun  we have about 200 students in this class  mostly freshmen and sophomores it's fun  to teach a class where last spring most  of these kids were in high school  I love the being able to bring global  affairs to a new audience what about the  other three  the other three segments sure so you  have the the second segments ongoing  right now it's on US energy policy and  gnash use me energy policy and national  security and we have Jim Woolsey the  former director of the CIA under  President Clinton teaching that he's  great he was a courtroom attorney for 22  years prior to some of his other many  jobs and he's been fascinating the third  module leaves the United States  altogether and will look at policy  around hiv/aids in sub-saharan Africa  professor Nicola Nattrass from the  University of Cape Town will teach that  module and then the last module will be  Richard Goldstone mr. Goldstone is the  author of the so-called Goldstone report  on the US excuse me on the conflict in  Gaza he's also known perhaps less  perhaps less well known but equally  important he was the chief prosecutor  for the Rwandan and the Bosnian genocide  trials and he'll be doing a module on  the role of international law  wonderful how where do you see the the  Jackson Institute in the next ten years  what are your aspirations for it  Wow Marilyn so we've been open for two  and a half weeks we got a spot ten years  feels like a while I would love to have  just an incredible undergraduate major  it's well established and that is  drawing great Yale college students who  are going to go on and really impact the  world around them and it's a major I  think that will equip them to do exactly  that I'd like to have a world class  masters degree program and I'd like to  continue I think  we're off to a great start with our  Jackson senior fellows I want to keep  that rolling thank you so much for being  here with us today oh it's my pleasure  thank you so much for more information  about Professor Levinson and the Jackson  Institute please visit our website at  yale.edu backslash McMillan report be  sure to join us again for another  episode of the McMillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty McMillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale  you
 

